  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does wind make a wildfire spread?",
      "a": "Wind brings fresh oxygen, pushes the flames forward onto unburnt plants, and carries hot embers ahead. Fires can move many times faster in strong wind.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is wind dangerous in a fire?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do fires race uphill?",
      "a": "Heat rises, and on a slope the flames lean towards the plants above, drying and heating them before the fire arrives. Firefighters say spread speed roughly doubles with every 10 degrees of slope.",
      "asks": [
        "Do fires spread faster up hills?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are embers and spot fires?",
      "a": "Embers are burning bits of bark, leaves and twigs lifted by the fire's hot air. Wind can carry them a kilometre or more ahead, where they land and start new fires called spot fires.",
      "asks": [
        "How do fires jump over roads?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a firebreak?",
      "a": "A firebreak is a strip of ground cleared of plants, or a road or river, that a fire cannot cross easily because there is no fuel. Firefighters also burn strips on purpose ahead of a fire.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you stop a wildfire?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are wildfires worse in hot, dry weather?",
      "a": "Heat and drought dry out grass, leaves and wood, so they need less heat to catch fire. Dry fuel lights quickly and burns fiercely.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do droughts cause wildfires?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Embers carried by strong winds can start new spot fires a kilometre or more ahead of the main fire.",
    "A common firefighting rule of thumb says a fire's speed roughly doubles for every 10 degrees of upward slope."
  ],
